{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994445,"nonce":"/3+z+q2eQDqvLQwbHA/3k32BbDc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrbLg","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/202","origin":"github"},"title":"fem.solve(adapt=) on a steady problem takes no linear=, nonlinear= or precond= slot","message":"On a steady problem, `fem.solve(adapt=jno.solve.remesh(...))` accepts no `linear=`, `nonlinear=` or `precond=` slot. An adaptive steady solve can only run on the default solver, or with a hand-written `solve_fn`.\n\nReproduced on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22).\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jno\n\noutline = [(0, 0), (1.0, 0), (1.0, 0.5), (0.5, 0.5), (0.5, 1.0), (0, 1.0)]\nd = jno.shape.polygon(outline, size=0.1).domain()\nu, phi = d.fem_symbols()\nxi, yi, _ = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)\nxb, yb, _ = d.variable(\"boundary\", split=True)\nui, vi = u.bind(x=xi, y=yi), phi.bind(x=xi, y=yi)\nfem = jno.fem([ui.x * vi.x + ui.y * vi.y - 1.0 * vi, u(xb, yb) - 0.0])\nadapt = jno.solve.remesh(theta=0.5, max_iters=2, refine_factor=2.0)\nfem.solve(adapt=adapt)                           # runs\nfem.solve(adapt=adapt, linear=jno.solve.lu())    # raises\n```\n\n```\nNotImplementedError: fem.solve: adapt= composes with the nonlinear=/linear=/precond= slots only on a transient\nproblem; x0= (warm start) and time= do not compose with remeshing (the DOF layout changes across a remesh). Drop\nthem, or pass solve_fn= for a custom loop.\n```\n\n## Why it matters\n\n- The choice of linear solver is the same decision on an adapted mesh as on a fixed one. A sparse direct backend (`lu(backend=...)`) or a preconditioned Krylov method cannot be selected for a steady adaptive solve.\n- The tutorials work around it with a private function: `adaptive_l_shape.py` and `soft_enrich_l_shape.py` pass `solve_fn` built on `jno.utils.solver.linear.sparse_lu_solve`. A user who copies the tutorial depends on a private import.\n\n## Request\n\nLet the steady adaptive loop take the `linear=`, `nonlinear=` and `precond=` slots, as the transient one does. The slot objects do not depend on the DOF layout, so they can be applied again after each remesh. The tutorials can then drop the private import.","files":null}]}